1
00:00:00,110 --> 00:03:21,650
Part 12 stopped on the example of the fog. What can darkness made of fog do to a night that is already dark. One example has been given. The second is the glow of a firefly in the day. When the sun's light is spread, what will a firefly's light do. The sun is up, and one firefly tries to shine so brightly that the sun's eye is dazzled. In the day one cannot even tell whether it has light. It does not appear. In the same way, before the Lord's power, Brahma and Shiva are really nothing. Darkness like fog in darkness, and a firefly's ray in the day. Two examples. If someone of a small power uses that power on a greatly powerful person, his own power of lordship is covered. His ability is destroyed. The great person is not overcome. Brahma used his power on the Lord. I know the four Vedas. My place is in Satyaloka. After the Lord, I am the one. Let me put them in a circle and see how they weep. They will go weeping through Braj, our children have been stolen. The Lord was eating the children's remnants. He wanted to make him weep a little and take pleasure. Where was the weeping. Brahma himself fell into the circle. He could put no effect. Srila Shukadeva Goswami is giving a warning. People of a small power should not use their power on a great person. Otherwise the power that was already there is also destroyed.

2
00:03:25,620 --> 00:06:47,750
In that ashram a saint lived. Someone took shelter. Gurudeva, a cat is troubling me badly. I have to stay in the hole. I am not eating, I am not moving about. She snarls there. The mahatma asked, what do you want. The cat was pouncing. We are in great trouble, please arrange something. The mahatma said, all right, become a cat. He became a cat. The fear of the cat was gone, but now dogs began to pounce. He took shelter again. The mahatma said, become a dog. He became a dog. Now the cow ran to strike him, and the bull too. Guruji, I am suffering greatly from these animals. All right, you become a cow. He became a cow. Then he says, a tiger is restless over me. All right, become a tiger. He became a tiger. He says to his own guru, now I am hungry. You have made my nature that of a tiger, so now I want to eat you. He wanted to use his power on the guru. The guru said, become a mouse again. The moment he heard it, become a mouse again. He remained a mouse's mouse. One should not imitate great persons. Even by mistake, one should not try to overcome them. I have surpassed them, I will press them down. Then one's own power is lost. It destroys the lordship in oneself. The opulence that is in oneself, the quality of being lord, that itself is destroyed. Brahma's own intelligence was lost. Now the intelligence was not working. What has happened.

3
00:06:48,550 --> 00:11:38,420
Now the real sight began. While they were still only thinking, fallen into worry, what shall we do, why did we do such a thing. Meanwhile all the calves and their herders appeared in a special way. How. Dense blue. The form of the calves changed at once. The same body, like a dark cloud. Dressed in yellow silk. This form of the Lord is well known in the Vedas and among the people too. Ask even a child, what is the color of the Lord's body. He will say, blue. We ask little children, and they say it at once. And those who have read a little philosophy, ask them, and they say the Lord has no color. The mind has been given away. By reading more they have found out that there is no color. Yes, they have read further. The Lord's color should be put far away. What does he hold in the hand. Children tell at once, the flute. Whatever language they know. What does he keep on the head. A peacock feather. Which animal stands with the Lord. The cow. It is not that a pig stands with the Lord. The Lord could keep any animal, but the one who is really the Lord has cows standing in his lila. The Lord does not keep a dog either. He plays the flute, he wears the peacock feather, he tends the cow. This is the Lord. A blue body, and a yellow cloth. All the boys and calves began to appear so to Brahma. A beautiful crown on every head. Earrings at the ears. A necklace of jewels at the throat. And with it a forest garland, a garland of flowers. The mark of Srivatsa on every chest. Because all of them were the Lord. They were not living beings. All had become Krishna. The Kaustubha jewel. A neck like a conch. Bracelets on the hands, anklets on the feet, the thread at the waist, rings on the fingers. The Lord stays decorated in this way with every ornament, always.

4
00:11:39,380 --> 00:17:10,880
And a new garland of tulasi, filling them from the feet to the head. Soft on every limb. Hanging down to the feet of all, a completely new tulasi garland. Now tell me, who gave so many garlands, made and put on at once. The Lord is wearing them. On all of them the garland is new. Who offered it. Those who have fulfilled a great deal, through very many births. They become the Lord's associates. They make the garland and offer it. Devotees keep offering garlands. In Vaikuntha there are endless crores of the Lord's associates. The Lord is one, but the associates are endless. Their number is at least three times this material world. They stay in service. A great fullness. Not as many as the living beings in one universe, three times that. Filled with persons who have fulfilled much. They have done much bhajan, much devotion. Then one day the chance comes, of the Lord's service, of putting on the garland, of waving the chamara, of waving the fan. The worshipped forms set up on this earth give the living being a chance to do the work that is to be done in Vaikuntha. Soft garlands had been put on. Sometimes in this world people make a garland very hard. They do not consider that it will prick the Lord's limb. Soft on every limb. On any limb that garland felt very soft. The Lord is very gentle. The smile is being called moonlight. A clear smile, spreading like the ray of the moon. Each one, and what is the use of still saying calf, all are the Lord's own form. How pleased the Lord stays. A smile keeps scattering from the lotus face. The face is like the moon. Whoever sees it sinks in bliss. As the moon rises and removes the heat by cool rays, so on seeing the face all sorrow goes far. And the eyes were reddish. Shukadeva says, Brahma saw redness in the eyes, and a smile in a face more beautiful than the moon. The smile of the face is doing the work of goodness, and the redness of the eye looks like passion. It seems the Lord is creating and also maintaining. Creating what. The purposes of his own people. Creating the desires in the devotees' minds, maintaining them, and fulfilling them. By the sight a desire is born in the heart, that the Lord accept our service, that he look upon us. By passion he was also bringing them forth, and by goodness he was maintaining them. Beautiful verses are being given here. In a week of the Bhagavatam a formality has to be completed, so we are going ahead.

5
00:17:10,880 --> 00:24:02,590
But Shukadeva is saying, creation and maintenance. Even without seeing the Lord, desires do arise in devotees. After the seeing, endless desires arise. These are called his own people's purpose. From Brahma down to a blade of grass. Every living being, moving and unmoving, with dance, song, and many different gifts, was worshipping separately. The perfections, becoming small, becoming light, and the rest, had taken form and were serving. The opulences, the order, the maya, and the rest, were serving. The twenty-four elements also, having taken form. Among the twenty-four, the mahat and the rest. Kapila counted them. Mind, ego, the mahat, and nature. Some place intelligence between mind and ego, and make twenty-five. Some count the three qualities apart and make twenty-eight. For the learned, nothing in this is unbecoming. However they count, it is fine. Uddhava asked, some say twenty, some twenty-six, some twenty-four, some twenty-five. The Lord says, sometimes they count two as one, sometimes they take three or four out of one. It is a way of counting. In the main there are twenty-four elements, the material elements. Time, nature, impression, desire, and karma. All of these were serving. The meaning is that Brahma saw, all of these are his energies. Time, which is called the disturber. It produces a stir in the qualities of nature. Nature, as a thing's own way. As the nature is, so the result. A tulasi leaf comes out green. Some tree's leaf is red, some is large. There is a small difference between impression and nature. As the impression is, so one will act. Of the stored karma, which karma is being started now. Impression has two meanings. Spice is mixed in and it is made worse, or its effect is lessened. As when vegetables are brought from the market, they say, prepare this, peel it, cut it. Here the meaning is, the fruit of karma will affect the mind, but by nature, by company, that karma becomes of some special kind. Good or bad company sharpens the nature a little. A snake's nature is to bite. By some company, or by some herb, the tendency to bite is not there now, so we say, under the impression, it is not biting. Or it begins to bite more. Desire, change, good work, the qualities of nature. Before the Lord's glory the glory of all these was set aside. He is seeing endless great elements. Every form was true, eternal, dense knowledge, and nothing but endless bliss. The single form of the rasa of bliss. So much so that scholars who read the Upanishads, who know the purport of the Upanishads, are also unable to describe his glory.

6
00:24:03,790 --> 00:29:14,910
His great glory is untouched. Those who see by the eye of the Upanishads beyond this world, scholars transparent in Vedanta, also cannot touch his glory. In this way Brahma is seeing all the boys and the calves in this form of the Lord. By one sight, the unborn one saw all of them as the Supreme. Brahma saw that all are in the form of Sri Krishna alone, appeared from Sri Krishna alone, and Sri Krishna alone is in all these forms. He by whose light, by whose power, this whole world is lit and made, that same supreme person. Seeing all of them in the form of Vishnu, and in a form of such brilliance, what became of Brahma. From extreme wonder the eleven senses were stunned. Stopped. Standing as if fixed. He was not standing. He was mounted on the swan, seated on his carrier. But all the senses went slack. By that splendor. By the light of as many forms of the Lord as he was seeing. As it seems, there is some goddess of the village, and a little doll is lying before the goddess. As children today play with a small plastic doll. Or as someone makes a lifeless thing with four heads and stands it up. The eyelid does not fall, the hand does not move, because there is no breath. Brahma was like that on his carrier. It seemed a little girl had come playing before the goddess of the village in the east, and had pulled up the doll and set it down. Shukadeva says this to describe the stillness. He was struck dumb. No speech was coming out. He was not even able to offer praise. Not even a syllable. Stunned. Thus. In this way, when this was the condition of Brahma, the starter of Sarasvati. Seeing the Lord's glory he was thinking, what is this that is happening. What is happening before me. These calves appeared first, then in the form of cowherds, and now all have become the form of Vishnu. Into the endless glory, into the Lord who cannot be argued out, he had gone to show his own maya. He whom Vedanta describes, who is completely different from the world, and who is his own evidence. Then the unborn Supreme, Sri Krishna, drew away the curtain of his maya. He drew away one energy. As many as had been made in the form of Vishnu, he now ended them all.

7
00:29:14,910 --> 00:36:42,790
He had become like the dead. Speech was not coming. Nothing was being seen. The hand was not moving. He rose like the dead. It is not being said that he had died. But as someone rises after dying, he rose in that way. Shukadeva makes this increase of the telling. Inside, consciousness was there. But outside, now he obtained consciousness. The whole form of Vishnu had disappeared. The Lord had removed the maya. Then the eyes were recovered toward the near. Now his attention went outside. He began to see with the eye. Slowly it seemed, of hand and foot, that he had died and become alive again. And with difficulty he opened the sight. Eight eyes. Two in each head. He opened eight eyes, with great difficulty. And this, together with himself. He saw this along with himself. This, whom. Shukadeva is sitting in Braj and telling the katha, from the mind. See this, Braj, and himself. And in that same Braj he saw all around at once. He looked at the directions, at what stood before him. He began to see. It seemed he had died and risen. When he had come as a devotee, and in this condition, one should compare, how much difference had happened. He looked on all four sides. What stood before him. A Vrindavan filled with trees that are the livelihood of people. The livelihood of human beings, or of living beings, such trees. Vrindavan appeared, very full. Fruit on them, flowers on them, good trees. And what was very dear to see. When he saw the form of Vishnu, Brahma did not become pleased. He became very sad. But when he saw Vrindavan, he became pleased. Shukadeva says, the Vrindavan in which there is no enmity, because the Lord lives here. Enmity by nature. In some living beings, from birth itself, by nature, a very fearful enmity stays. One dog sees another unknown dog, and runs to bite. A tiger sees a deer, and runs to eat. A snake is touched, and it bites. Among human beings also, quarrel happens from birth. But in Vrindavan he saw, in no living being is there ever any anger. The going aside of the tendency, the going far, the absence of wrath. About five hundred years ago it was so. Now enmity keeps growing. Now where will a tiger and a man live together. If man lives with man, that itself is enough. But the Vrindavan that is unseen by our eyes is still the same. Even the living beings who keep a natural enmity live with one another in love, like friends. And hunger does not come. Hunger also is an enemy, a fault. So the Lord has not kept the problem of hunger. There is no anger. People who become angry should understand, this is coming against the principle of Vrindavan, so how great an offense. In the same way there is no theft. No hunger, no thirst, no craving for the sense objects. Here there is only the craving for bhajan, for loving the Lord.

8
00:36:43,910 --> 00:43:20,340
The play of a child of the cowherd line, semen raised upward. The nondual Brahman, supreme, endless, of unfathomable knowledge. Searching on every side for the calves and the friends, from the body of a child. The highest one saw him, one, with a morsel in the hand. See the nondual Brahman in Vrindavan. What kind of Brahman. Supreme, endless, knowledge without a bottom. And how did he see him. With a morsel in the hand, a morsel of curd and rice. One. Alone. Not with the friends. This is Brahma's complete sight. He is really the Lord's son. The greatest scholar of the Veda. So now he is seeing the nondual Brahman, a morsel in the hand, alone. He appeared again in that same form. He was searching for the boys, and for the calves, here and there, mind agitated. The morsel of curd and rice stayed in the hand as it had been. The Lord did not eat. Search, or eat. Where was the leisure to eat. He was fallen into worry. Brahma sees, he is searching, in that same form. On every side. In the groves, in the caves, in the valleys, on the banks. Endless, supreme, without a second. But the real qualifier is this, the morsel in the hand. And from this one should understand, the flute tucked in the cloth at the belly, the stick pressed in the armpit, the horn taken. With a face joined to a very deep grief, he is searching. The moment he saw the Lord, Brahma jumped suddenly from the swan. To come into contact. I have made a great mistake, please forgive me. I have seen this lila, the charming glory. Seeing it, he quickly got down from his own carrier. Onto the earth. Dropping the body like a rod of gold. Seeing him in this form, searching in Vrindavan, he got down from his carrier, he jumped. The Lord is walking on the earth, and Brahma is riding above. I have offended, and after offending, staying in a high place did not feel right. So he jumped. Srila Shukadeva is calling the body a rod of gold. Brahma is fair exactly like gold. Limbs of heated gold. The body is like heated gold. Dropping well. Hands forward. He also has eight hands, but here he will have made only two. He will have hidden his own godhood. The four heads were still there. Feet put back. Or he began to roll in the dust like Akrura, so that dust would touch all four faces. Or, with the tips of the four crowns, a touch of the Lord's two feet. The feet were very light, very soft. The meaning of the touch is, he did not make them touch. He did not have the courage to touch the crown to the soft foot. In a way it seems the touch was meant, and it is touching the mark of the foot. This is the feeling.

9
00:43:20,850 --> 00:46:42,450
The Lord had stood still. He was doing such a play, it seemed he had become astonished. This one living being with four heads has come and stands like this before him. He was holding this feeling inside. He was completely astonished. The all-knowing Lord was in this way, and he did not speak to Brahma, right to the end. Brahma began to roll in this way and to weep. He dropped his own tears on the Lord's two feet. As if the feet were bathed. From the eight eyes, four times two, tears were coming. He was weeping by turns. Rising and rising, he fell again. Rising and rising, for a long time, falling at Krishna's feet. For a long time. He would rise, after a while. The Lord was not speaking, he was quiet. So he would rise again, see him, and fall again. If he falls again and again, perhaps the Lord will say, go, I forgive you, it is no matter. This is what he was thinking. But the Lord had become such a child that he simply does not speak. He does not even ask, where have you come from, why are you rolling. Brahma was weeping. He was falling between the feet. He stayed a long while. Remembering the glory seen before, that they had become the form of Vishnu, and all the glory, remembering it he fell again and again at the feet, and stood up again and again. For a long time. After that, when the Lord did not speak at all, he began the praise. Earlier he was not speaking, he was only weeping, falling at the feet. Slowly rising, wiping the eyes. After that he rose, and wiped the eyes. He wiped all eight eyes. He wiped the tears. And seeing Mukunda, the neck bent. Shoulder lowered. Hands joined. And with a choked voice. Gira means speech. With a choked voice Brahma began to praise the Lord. This praise is very well known. In the Bhagavatam it is a praise for making the great, complete principle. It is a very beautiful praise. We are taking its first verse.

10
00:46:43,190 --> 00:51:56,500
I praise your body that is like a cloud, your cloth that is like lightning. Your face shining with a gunja earring and a peacock feather. The beauty of a forest garland, and of the marks of the morsel, the stick, the horn, and the flute. Soft feet, born from the body of the cowherd. First of all Brahma is offering obeisance. What he has experienced until now in his life, who in this world is the supreme truth beyond all, toward which form the love is, who has seized this mind. The praise of that form. He had seen the cowherd friends as the form of the Lord, and he did not praise there. But the Brahman whose lila is astonishing, and who has put the greatest effect on the heart, choosing that form, the obeisance is to be made. With the word naumi the accusative would apply. But this is the dative. In devotion he is offering obeisance. Sripada Jiva Goswami writes, the dative in the sense of the accusative. This dative has been given in place of the accusative. It would be te namah, if the word namah were there. To the cloud-body, or to you. With naumi the accusative applies. I praise the son of Nanda. But if namah remains, then namah to the son of Nanda. Brahma is saying naumi first of all, because the feeling of obeisance is first of all. Worthy of praise. There is no occasion here for someone else worthy of having qualities sung. O Lord, in this world you alone are worthy of praise. Your qualities alone should be sung. I offer you obeisance. To the cloud-body. To whom. To him whose body is like a cloud. Abhra means a cloud. A cloud joined with water looks black, dark. Whose body is like a dark cloud. And to him whose cloth is like lightning. The body like a cloud, the cloth like lightning. In this there is the feeling, O Lord, rain a little of the nectar of words. As a cloud rains, so you also rain something. Otherwise I will die like this. Say something. Be kind to us. The ears are restless. He had come, and he was in his own grief. He is expressing this feeling. You are a cloud-body, so be kind, rain the nectar of mercy.

11
00:51:58,290 --> 00:57:43,660
Someone may say, a body like a cloud is also Lord Vishnu's, and he also wears a yellow cloth. Are you offering obeisance to him. Then he says, no. To the face shining with a gunja earring and a surrounding peacock feather. Whose earrings are also like an ornament of gunja. These earrings. Earrings strung of the fruits. The Lord did not want to wear earrings of jewels. He would tie the forest gunja at the ear. He would hang the earring. So, whose ear-ornament is gunja. Both ears. And the surrounding feather. The peacock's feather. To the shining face. Whose face is looking very beautiful. To such a Lord the obeisance is to be made. As was read before, the Lord was decorating with the children, and he is decorating himself too. So, describing the very decoration he has just done in Vrindavan, the very form he is in, he is offering obeisance. If the Lord is a store of beauty, still by the peacock feather and by the gunja his face is looking better. The Lord's beauty is increased, as Uddhava does, the ornament ornaments the limb. But by the gunja of Vrindavan and by the peacock feather, Sri Krishna's face is shining, beautiful, looking more beautiful. To such a Lord I offer obeisance. To him of the forest garland. And how. To the beauty of the marks of the morsel, the stick, the horn, and the flute. The morsel of curd and rice. The morsel, the stick, the horn, and the flute. By those, the mark, the beauty, whose it is, to him. By these special things his beauty is increasing. Above also he is saying, by the gunja and by the peacock feather the face looks beautiful. And he is wearing these four things, so he looks more beautiful. The morsel, the stick, the horn, and the flute. Lakshma means a mark. By these four marks, by these four ornaments, the beauty has grown. A morsel of curd and rice in the hand. The flute at the waist. In the two armpits, the stick on one side and the horn on the other. And to him born from the body of the cowherd. Of the tender of animals, of Sri Nanda, born of the body. Obeisances to you, the son of Nanda the king. The son of him who tends the animals. Anga means the body. Son of Sri Nanda Maharaj, I offer you obeisance. He is bringing it completely at the end. Not in a reserved sense. The gunja and the peacock feather, the other boys had also worn. In order to say Sri Krishna in particular. Or he is offering obeisance including the friends. All of them also, gunja at the ear, a shining face, and they also born of the cowherd's body. All. It goes in the singular. Why was the obeisance not given in the plural, if he is offering it to the cowherd friends. In the mind, he is offering it to them too. But for the kind, the singular.

12
00:57:43,660 --> 01:01:23,260
Before this he has done a great deal of prayer to the form of Vishnu and the form of Narayana. And to Lord Krishna also. Govinda, the original person, him I worship. Nondual, infallible, without beginning, of endless form. All of this he has said. Of Yashoda, of the cowherds, he has done a great increase. But now he is telling the essential experience of his own life. I am offering obeisance to this form of yours. Of this form alone may I remain the eternal servant. In this lila of yours may my taste be. In my eyes, in the mind, in the speech, may you stay spread in this form. This is the prayer. To say this, giving confidence again and again, he is offering obeisance. And before the Lord that same form is there, so he offers it. Soft feet. I forgot, where I am. Whose two feet are exceedingly soft. The Lord was a child. Even then, in Vrindavan, he did not go to herd the calves with shoes on. I considered, the calves are our worshipable Lord. I am going as a servant, so I will not go with shoes on. He is seeing those soft feet. With those same soft feet he was searching for the children in the valley, the grove, the cave. Soft feet. And who is the son of the cowherd, the son of Nanda Maharaj. To such a Lord I am offering obeisance. He is not saying the word Prabhu. He is applying only words of sweetness. And this itself he is calling the praise. The Lord's qualities should be sung, but of which form, of which lila, that he is telling. Simply, born of the cowherd's body. In the relation of a friend they are the guru of the world, the greatest, Brahma really is, in this universe. But the realization that is his, that is the real realization. That should be understood. It is our very great fortune that we are in Brahma's line. Knowledge of the Lord has been given. And the one to be worshipped is Sri Krishna, Sri Krishna who wanders Vrindavan, in this form. In the form of the son of Nanda Maharaj. The son of the king of Braj is the one to be worshipped. In this way this session has come near its completion.

13
01:01:26,360 --> 01:06:02,270
In the middle I was of a mind to change, not to tell this lila, to tell some further principle, choosing the year. But the devotees insisted, no, no, this itself should be. I wanted to draw back a little, because I had not read this. But the Lord whose katha I am telling does everything. By Lord Krishna's kindness his lila was told before you. I experienced it. What I want to say, the Lord, seated in the heart, is making it appear. The thing that was remembered, that also comes to memory. Or say, you are the company of devotees. You have come to hear talk of Krishna, so by the effect of your devotion it appears. I had completely decided that I would not tell it. There was another reason too. I had the commentary on the Bhagavatam up to the eleventh chapter. From the twelfth, the commentary was not with me. Then I was thinking, I have told that, so how will I tell this without a commentary. But when it was settled that no, it must be told, then I remembered. Let me go and look in the library. On going there, the commentary was found. All of you gather every year to hear. There is no doubt in this. The person who is eager to hear is a greater devotee than the speaker, and fortunate. Because in the telling, the teller today tells even for honor. This is a natural thing. Many people stay eager to tell, to sing, because applause comes, praise comes. But to be eager to hear, that inspiration comes only from pure devotion. If there is devotion, then a person will want to hear. You have devotion. In the heart there is devotion to Krishna. The Lord gave company in this way, in his own abode. A chance came, of all you devotees, and of sitting in the assembly and telling the line of the Lord's qualities. For this I count myself very fortunate. Ah, we are the most fortunate in the world of men. A chance was given in this way. First, on this occasion of Janmashtami, I want to thank Krishna alone. And after that, Krishna's devotees, by whose effort everything happened, the program happened, all the arrangement was made. In an arrangement, a miss still remains a miss. But you tolerated it. No very heavy complaint came. This also is a very good thing. We promise, next year we will improve still more.

14
01:06:03,580 --> 01:11:06,050
The devotees of Bombay had prayed a great deal of us. You speak for several years in Vrindavan, so this year please come to Mira Road. I said, no, I cannot say it from my own side. If that great Prabhu says yes, all right, then it is all right. Otherwise not. This is an auspicious day. When the Lord appears in this world, some people see him directly, hear him, meet him. It is auspiciousness and nothing but auspiciousness. Even before the Lord's coming, auspiciousness had covered every place, while he was in the womb. The translation of this is in the Ramcharitmanas. The day the Lord was in the womb, that same day auspiciousness covered the whole world. The auspiciousness he will do after taking the avatar, that he will of course do. But the lilas he does after taking the avatar, and those lilas, Vedavyasa and the other devotees, the great souls, write. Now you may go, you may return to your own abode. Dharma has been established. The work of the gods has been done. Everyone came to Dvaraka. The Lord said one thing. Dharma has been established, but I am here, so ahead also people's minds will stay in our devotion. Thinking this, the Lord went to his own abode. Thinking this, that our fame is in this world. This is the question, I bow, I take shelter. The sages'. When Krishna has now gone to his own abode, along with dharma and knowledge. Whose shelter has dharma gone to. The Lord went to his own abode along with dharma and knowledge. Now who in this universe will protect dharma. The mark of dharma is devotion. The Lord himself has told it. Who will protect awareness of Krishna, devotion to the Lord, in the Lord's absence. Then, by himself, like a great magnet, like a greatly powerful magnet, he draws all living beings. Even a creeper, a leaf, a living being that is a root, loves him. Animals and birds do, human beings do. But when he had gone. Those who hear this, who read this, their good will be the same as of those who are living with Krishna. Several devotees have said so. The Lord's avatar happens in order to do lila. Five thousand years ago the lila was done. And every year people keep celebrating the Lord's avatar. This festival is celebrated in the whole world. In India, from before. And of all the festivals, this one is being celebrated by the purest method. In today's age also it is very dear, on Janmashtami.

15
01:11:33,390 --> 01:13:39,250
They come to the Lord's temple. Yesterday I saw, people were coming into the temple, a very large number. And not only this, in all the temples. Radha Vallabha, Banke Bihari, Rangji. Then a thought rose in the mind. People have a natural faith toward Lord Krishna. A natural faith. So I was thinking, what is our duty. If they have faith, the duty is to give them the pure message of the Lord. The right thing, which is for their good. As there is the katha of the Bhagavatam, as there is kirtan. All of this should be given. A program of this kind on Janmashtami, this really is the duty of us sadhus. Do katha, do kirtan, tell about the Lord. This, not a blow, a gift. Their tendency is there, toward the Lord. But the poor people think, we have had darshan, we have given something to the Lord, and with that much they become satisfied. Love is there, but the love does not become fully developed. It has to be developed. The aarti of the most pure Purana. The mine of dharma, devotion, and knowledge. The Mahapurana, the Bhagavatam, fallen from the mouth of Sri Shuka. At every moment the nectar of the highest bliss. The churning of the dirt of Kali. The end of the fear and the feeling of birth and death. Serving always, the maker of every happiness. The aarti of knowing the character of the great king. Of the most pure Purana. The one that bewilders the desire for the sense objects. A spotless desire. The heap of attaining the Lord. The supreme light, knowledge of the Supersoul. The paramahamsa sage, the mind delighted. Glory to the Mahapurana, for those who have faith. The sitting is completed here. This is the last existing part of Vatsalya Lila.
